  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on a self-selected sample within a single region, which may limit generalizability to other cultural or geographic contexts.",
      "what": "This study maps the spatial experiences of 140 self-identified bisexual individuals out of 1,009 total survey participants in Catalonia, Spain. Using the ReliefMaps model to integrate quantitative and qualitative data, the research measures spatial discomfort across five key domains: home, public space, leisure, virtual spaces, and healthcare.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that bisexual spatial discomfort stems from a lack of social intelligibility and monosexual assumptions rather than explicit biphobia, offering a new framework for how geographies of sexuality conceptualize non-monosexual identities."
